// =============================================================================================
// p50 / p95 / p99 by endpoint.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHY PERCENTILES AND NOT AN AVERAGE
//
// An average hides exactly the thing you need to see. Take 99 requests at 20 ms and one at 5 s:
// the mean is 70 ms, which looks healthy and is a number no single user experienced. The p99 is
// 5 s, which is what the unluckiest user in a hundred actually waited.
//
// p50 and p99 together say something an average cannot. p50 is the typical experience; p99 is the
// tail. When they are close the service is predictable. When p99 is an order of magnitude above
// p50 something is intermittent — a cold cache, a lock, a retry, a GC pause — and the median will
// never show it.
//
// p95 is included because the gap between p95 and p99 tells you how many requests are in the
// tail. If both are high the slowness is broad; if only p99 is high it is a small number of
// outliers, which is a different investigation.
// =============================================================================================

requests
| where timestamp > ago(1h)

// Only the API role. The worker maps no endpoints, so anything it reported here would be a
// health probe, and averaging two roles that do different work produces a number describing
// neither.
| where cloud_RoleName == 'quotes-api'

// /health is excluded deliberately. It is called by probes far more often than the real
// endpoints, it does almost nothing, and leaving it in drags every aggregate towards its latency
// — making the service look faster than any user ever experiences.
| where name !has 'health'

| summarize
    calls    = count(),
    failures = countif(success == false),
    p50      = round(percentile(duration, 50), 1),
    p95      = round(percentile(duration, 95), 1),
    p99      = round(percentile(duration, 99), 1),
    max      = round(max(duration), 1)
  by endpoint = name

// The ratio is the actionable column. A p99/p50 near 1 means predictable; 10 means something
// intermittent is happening that the median cannot see.
| extend tailRatio = round(todouble(p99) / iff(p50 == 0, 1.0, todouble(p50)), 1)
| extend errorRatePct = round(100.0 * failures / calls, 1)

| project endpoint, calls, p50, p95, p99, max, tailRatio, failures, errorRatePct
| order by p99 desc
